//! The words a model reads: Go `mcpsrv/text.go` and the queue tool's, verbatim (Go `fmt` templates: `%[n]s`,
//! `%[n]d`, filled by `fill`). Generated from the Go source; tools/list pins them byte for byte.

pub const INSTRUCTIONS: &str = r####"BeatScript: AI-assisted algorithmic composition. BeatScript is also the name of its language: small, total and deterministic, for writing whole music tracks as text. A program has one line per deck, parts joined with |, 16 steps a bar, sections and an arrange; the same canonical text always has the same CID. Programs are CC0 by default.

You write the programs: there is no compose tool. The authoring loop:
1. Read %[1]s/docs/cheatsheet.md (the compact grammar) and one or two programs from list_examples (pass name for the full text).
2. Call instruments for the voices and kits you may name.
3. For vocals, consider a phrasebook line chosen for its meter (list_examples with phrasebook true, or genre_template with phrase): see %[1]s/docs/found-phrases.md. For a shape, consider a classical form built from public-domain themes (genre_template with form, or list_examples with themes true): see %[1]s/docs/forms.md.
4. Write the program in BeatScript 2: its first line is `#! beatscript 2`.
5. Call check and fix every error at its line:col; repeat until ok is true.
6. Call listen to hear it as numbers: every deck and part that has notes but is not heard (and why), loudness per section, the tempo and key heard, clipping, and the mix: each part's bands, the clashes between parts, the true peak and the loudness against a target. Fix what its warnings say, in their order, one line at a time, and listen again with compare (the previous version's CID) to see what each edit fixed and what it broke: %[1]s/docs/polish.md.
7. Play it with eval (your text) or load (a stored program) in a browser tab open at %[1]s, and store it with share (a link; signed in it also stores the program). render gives audio you can open. After eval, get's warnings say if a deck is inaudible on that tab (crossfader, mute, level, filter). What you send with eval, load, queue, share or push_private is formatted on the way in, with your `#!` header lines and comments kept and its version as you wrote it, so it arrives tidy with the same CID.
8. To show a program as a page the person can press Play on (a claude.ai Artifact, or any page host), call artifact and publish its html exactly as it is: do not hand-roll a player or rewrite the page. For a stored program it carries the site's render in the page (audio inline, nothing loaded); for a text, or with audio engine, it plays live with the BeatScript player pinned on cdn.jsdelivr.net by version and Subresource Integrity. To put a program anywhere else (any web page, an iframe, a README, a notebook, VS Code, an RSS feed, the Android app), or to give the person the whole studio in an Artifact (the editor, its checker and the player, its tools running in the page), call embed with a target and an embed config (%[1]s/embed-config.schema.json) and use its snippet or page as it is; with target artifact, declare the capabilities it answers when you publish the page.
Gotcha: a voice name that starts with a digit, such as 808-bass, cannot be written: inst=808-bass reads as the sum 808 - bass, and quoting it ("808-bass") is refused too. Pick a voice that starts with a letter instead (for a deep bass, inst=sub-bass).
Versions: a program whose header has the line `#! beatscript 2` is BeatScript 2, the language the docs teach; write that. A text with `#! btw 1`, or with no version line, is BeatScript 1, the old spelling: it still checks and plays, and keeps its old CID. check says which (version, 1 or 2) and gives renamed_cid, the same program's CID in the other version. fmt upgrades a BeatScript 1 text to BeatScript 2 (a new CID; from_cid is the old one); nothing else upgrades. Old CIDs keep working, and %[1]s/p/<old CID> redirects to the upgraded program once the site has it.
The full language is %[1]s/docs/BEATSCRIPT.md; everything is indexed at %[1]s/llms.txt.
Source: https://github.com/stackdump/beatscript (MIT); programs CC0.

Tools that write (eval, load, stop, queue) need a bearer token: sign in on %[1]s and press "Create an MCP token". The rest work without one. A phone paired with the account (devices) is driven by passing its id as device to eval, load, get or stop. queue keeps an "up next" list of stored programs on a tab; it never writes music, and it advances only while someone is listening."####;

pub const CHECK_DESC: &str = r####"Check a BeatScript program without playing it: parse, type-check and compile the text. ALWAYS call check before eval, share or render, and fix every error at its line:col before trying again: a program with an error is refused whole. Warnings do not block. A `control:` line is the control deck (docs/BEATSCRIPT.md "The control deck"): engine calls only (move, swap pairs, load pair, queue, crossfader, a timed tempo, duck, mix, do), beside deck A:, deck B: and master:, sequenced by sections like a deck; it checks and plays as the same line with no head. An `arrange { }` switches it pass by pass with a line of its own: `~ control off` (a section's control-deck lines do not fire from here on), `~ control on`, `@ control off` (the next section only), `~ control freeze off` (the lines of one call), `~3 control off` (every 3rd pass); plays[].control_off in the answer's arrange names the calls each play holds back.

Answers {ok, errors, warnings, diagnostics[{severity, line, col, end_col, message, expected, found, help, render}], cid, canonical, version, renamed_cid, expanded_cid, tempo, key, sections, song_bars, seconds, evolve, uses_assets, restricted, docs}, and refs and resolution for a program with references, and arrange {passes, unbounded, plays[{section, name, pass, coda, start_bar, bars, reads, control_off}], patterns, fires} for an `arrange { }` with mutations. docs is the text's doc comments, [{kind, name, doc}]: the `#` lines directly above a section, let, def or arrange (a comment, so the CID does not change); sections[].doc is a section's, when it has one. line and col are 1-based; help says what would fix it ("did you mean ..."); render is the same error in caret form. ok:false is an answer, not a tool error. cid and canonical are of the canonical text (comments and spacing do not change the CID). version is the language version: 2 for a text whose header has `#! beatscript 2` (write that), 1 for a text with `#! btw 1` or no version line (BeatScript 1, the old spelling, which checks as before and keeps its old CID); renamed_cid is the CID of the same program in the other version (fmt upgrades a BeatScript 1 text to it). A station program (radio: a `station { }` block, see the station tool) checks the tracks of its first pass of forms and its first segue, and the answer gains station {text, cid, genre, generator, seed, step, forms, tempo, key, length, start_cid, version, tracks, segue}.

With same_as (another program's CID or text) the answer gains same_as {same, text_same, basis, why, diffs[{section, deck, start_bar, a_cid, b_cid}], only_in_a, only_in_b, renamed, song, diff}: whether the two are the same song by their compiled nets and song-level settings, not their text. When they are not, diff says what changed in music: diff.summary is one line per change ("tempo 124 -> 126", "moved outro: bar 13 -> bar 5", "drop2 bass = drop bass transposed +1", "drop bass: 1 note changed (1:7 c3 -> d3)"), with song, arrangement (inserted, removed, moved, repeated, renamed, changed) and parts (a transform: transpose, octave, rotate, reverse, thin, fill, fast, slow; else the notes added, removed or changed at bar:step) as JSON. Use it to prove a refactor changed nothing, or to find what an edit did.

This is the loop for writing a program (there is no compose tool): read %[1]s/docs/cheatsheet.md and one or two list_examples programs, call instruments, write the program (its first line `#! beatscript 2`), call check and fix every line:col error until ok, then eval / load to play it and share to store it. Voices come from the instruments tool: use only names from it. For a sung line, a public-domain phrasebook line (list_examples with phrasebook true) comes with its meter, stress and phoneme escapes, so that sing @id on melody checks with no unknown-word warning (%[1]s/docs/found-phrases.md). A voice name with a leading digit (808-bass) cannot be written: inst=808-bass reads as 808 - bass and a quoted "808-bass" is refused, so pick a voice that starts with a letter (inst=sub-bass for a deep bass). Read-only, no sign-in. Limits: the text is at most %[2]d bytes; about %[3]d calls a minute."####;

pub const EXPAND_DESC: &str = r####"Write out every seed(...) and template(...) of a BeatScript program as the plain BeatScript it stands for, to start from a generated track and edit it. seed(genre, n[, structure]) is a track of the beats generator (genres: ambient blues bossa country dnb dubstep edm funk garage house jazz lofi metal reggae speedcore synthwave techno trance trap; structures: standard, loop, minimal, extended), and it is a value, never audio: `arrange from seed(techno, 42)` is the whole track, `section intro from seed(techno, 42)[1..8]` its first eight bars as a section, and `bass seed(techno, 42).bass[17..24]` (or `let riff = seed(techno, 42).bass[17..24]`) one part's bars as a pattern; seed@2(genre, n) is seeds v2, the same track with its genre's swing written (`arrange from seed@2(jazz, 7)` says swing 0.6; a bare seed(...) is seeds v1, written straight). template(name, phrase) compiles a phrase, a phrasebook id (@tempest_such_stuff) or any words in quotes, to a whole track, `arrange from template(deep-house, @tempest_such_stuff)` (templates: deep-house): the phrase is the vocal line, its syllables the line's length, its stress the accents and the beats, its meter the subdivision, and the arrangement is the template's own. A gen@N call (gen@2 techno seed=42 section=drop part=bass: the seeded generator, the program generate prints, pinned by its CID) is written out by where it stands: `arrange from gen@2 ...` as the whole program (under a `# from: gen@2 ... -> <cid>` line), a part line or `section X from gen@2 ... section=drop` as that section's parts, `let bed = gen@2 ... section=drop part=bass` as a let a bar (bed_1, bed_2, ..., bed = <bed_1 bed_2 ...>). check, eval and share take the references as they are and play the same music (the CID is of the short text); expand gives the long text, with a `# from seed(...)` or `# from template(...)` line over each place it wrote, to edit from there. Answers {ok, text, canonical, cid, from_cid, refs, resolution, diagnostics}: cid is the written-out program's, from_cid the one you gave; resolution is check's manifest (each reference once with its pin, and a template's nested parts, each with the CID of what it is written out as); with lineage true the text opens with `#! forked-from: <from_cid>`. With eject "line:col" it writes out only the reference there, in place, under its `# from: <call> -> <cid>` line (a comment: the ejected text has the CID of the same notes typed by hand), and answers {ok, text, cid, ejected: {call, kind, half, place, name, resolved, from}}. A `use SOURCE as NAME` line is written out as the definition its source makes (section NAME from ..., let NAME = ..., a theme's lets in the program's key), and a name in a role or a phrase's place as its source; the answer then also has imports, each name with its source. Read-only, no sign-in."####;

pub const GENERATE_DESC: &str = r####"Generate a whole BeatScript track from a seed, with no model and no sign-in: the beats generator picks the genre's rhythms (Euclidean), chord progression, instruments and song structure from the seed, and the result is printed as readable source (named lets, a def for the shared groove, sections, an arrange, filter ramps, the chords as Roman numerals and e()/arp()/markov()/every/rotate/degrade with their seeds written out, so one `key` line moves the whole song) for you to edit. Same genre, bpm, key, length and seed: the same text and CID, every time; the seed and generator are in the #! header so the track can be re-derived. The program passes check and is stored as public CC0 (source generate). Answers text, cid, name, seed, generator, url and render_url; play it with eval or load, or edit it and check again.

The answer also has gen, the call that is this program in BeatScript (gen@2 techno seed=42, with the bpm=, key= and length= you gave: `arrange from gen@2 techno seed=42` plays it), expanded_cid (the same as cid: a generated program has no references), and parts, so you can pick parts without reading the text: one entry a role, {role, section, sections, voice or kit, text, let, gen}, where gen is the call that is just that part (gen@2 techno seed=42 section=drop part=bass) and let is what `let drop_bass = <gen>` writes (a let a bar). Put a part's gen in a let or on a part line of your own program to layer it, mutate it in an arrange or cross it with another seed; there it is fitted to your program's key and tempo unless it says key= and bpm=, and check reports it in refs. With part (a role) the answer gains that part and its let alone; generator picks the version (1, 2, 3 or 4, as gen@1 to gen@4 name them; 3 writes BeatScript 2, 4 is 3 with the acid genre's bass on the 303); seeds (a list, or {count, start}, at most 16) answers a compact summary a seed instead of one program."####;

pub const FMT_DESC: &str = r####"Format a BeatScript program as the editor's fmt button does, in BeatScript 2: one statement a line, one space between tokens, blocks indented two spaces, numbers, notes and chords spelled one way, and the version line `#! beatscript 2` first; the other `#!` header lines, the comments and the blank lines between blocks stay where they are. A BeatScript 1 text (`#! btw 1`, or no version line) is upgraded: the same program under a new CID, and from_cid is the CID of the text as given (the old CID keeps working). Answers text (the formatted program), canonical (the same with no comments and no header lines but the version line: the CID is of that), cid, version (2), from_cid (only when it upgraded) and comments. A BeatScript 2 text formats to its own CID, so use fmt to tidy a program, to upgrade one, or to compare two; eval, load, queue, share and push_private already format what they are given and never upgrade it. A program that does not parse answers the same diagnostics as check. Read-only, no sign-in."####;

pub const EVAL_DESC: &str = r####"Send a program to a browser tab that has %[1]s open, as if the user pressed Cmd+Enter in the editor: the text replaces the editor's and the change lands on the next bar, with no gap; play:true starts playback too. Needs a bearer token.

A tab must be connected. It is the page's own connection, so ask the user to open %[1]s in a browser (signed in as the same account as the token, and eval reaches every live tab of that account), or, for a tab that is not signed in, to press MCP in the page's top bar and give you the session id it shows, which you pass as session. With no tab connected the answer is a no_session error that says this.

Run check first. eval checks too and refuses a program with errors (nothing is sent; the diagnostics come back), and one that uses samples or "use restricted" (the browser has no such files). The program is the user's to hear: do not eval over something they are playing without saying so. After eval call get to see what is playing, the section and bar, and any diagnostics the page itself reports. The text is formatted on the way in, as fmt does (header lines and comments kept), so the editor shows it tidy. Text is at most %[2]d bytes."####;

pub const LOAD_DESC: &str = r####"Put a stored or example program into a browser tab (the same tabs and rules as eval: a tab open at %[1]s, signed in as the token's account, or its session id from the page's MCP panel). Give exactly one of: cid (a program the site stored, or an example's CID; render and a signed-in share store theirs), example (a name from list_examples), url (a share link %[1]s/#CID.packed or a /c/ link; one made on an older host opens too; the link is verified, and one whose CID is not its text's is refused with cid_mismatch). play:true starts it. The program is formatted on the way in, as eval's text is. Needs a bearer token. Call get afterwards to see what is playing."####;

pub const GET_DESC: &str = r####"What a browser tab at %[1]s is doing now, as the page last reported it: the program text and cid, playing (true/false), source (engine or stream), section (name), section_num of sections_total, bar, bars_in_section, phase (0-7, the light in the phrase), marks (what is firing: active lines, token flashes, ramps and timed lines, with line:col spans into the text), diagnostics (the page's own check of the text), ts, mixer (what the listener hears: crossfader, master, and per deck its level, mute, EQ kills, filter, insert filter and vocoder; null from a page or phone too old to report it) and warnings (a playing deck that cannot be heard, and why: "deck B inaudible: the crossfader is all the way to A"). Use it after eval or load to confirm the change landed, and to see the program a user is playing and editing.

Needs a bearer token: it reads the tabs of the token's account, by default the most recently seen one. A tab that is not signed in is read by its session id (the page's MCP panel shows it), with your token. Read-only. No tab connected: a no_session error that says how to connect one."####;

pub const SHARE_DESC: &str = r####"Make the share links of a program. Always the self-sealing link: %[1]s/#<CID>.<base64url(deflate(formatted text))>; the program travels inside the link with its `#!` header lines and comments, and whoever opens it gets exactly that program, verified against the CID (the canonical text's: the headers and comments are not covered by it), and can play and fork it. Also social_url (%[1]s/c/<CID>.<packed>, the form to post where a link preview is wanted: it carries a card image). Called signed in (a bearer token), it also stores the program on the site (public, with its rights: license, forked_from, credits, name; the first share's rights stay) and returns the CID-only forms: short_url (%[1]s/p/<CID>) and short_social_url (%[1]s/c/<CID>), plus fetch_url, a public GET that returns the canonical text and rights for a phone or an app. A program with references (a template(...), a gen@N call, a seed(...), a borrowed <cid>.section) has expanded_cid, the CID of the same track with every reference written out as plain BeatScript; a signed-in share stores that expansion beside it, as a program of its own that plays the same (or finds it already stored: first write wins, so it keeps the rights its first sharer gave), and returns expanded_url (%[1]s/p/<expanded_cid>) and expansion_license. An expansion that would copy another's program under other rights (only CC0 programs, or the sharer's own under the same licence, are copied), or is over the size limit, is not stored, and expansion_note says why. Opening a short link fetches the text, recompiles it, recomputes the CID and refuses a mismatch. A short link to a BeatScript 1 program (%[1]s/p/<old CID>) redirects to its BeatScript 2 upgrade (the CID check's renamed_cid gives, the program fmt writes) once the site stores it; the old CID's text stays at fetch_url. Anonymous calls get the self-sealing links and short_url null. The program must pass check and must not use samples or "use restricted" (a private program is never shared or stored); programs shared here are CC0 unless license says otherwise. The text is formatted on the way in, its version kept (the stored program is its canonical text, whose CID the header lines and comments do not change, and the link carries the formatted text; a `#! name:` line names the stored program). Its `# from: <call> -> <cid>` lines (what an eject wrote out: comments, so not in the CID) are kept with it as meta.based_on ["<call> -> <cid>"]: its linked data lists each in isBasedOn as vendored, and lineage counts it (vendoredFrom); open with the CID answers them in meta. Links over about 2000 characters (chars says) may be cut by some apps; short links are not. A stored program also embeds in other sites: embed_url (%[1]s/embed/<CID>) is a player any page may frame, %[1]s/embed.js gives <beatscript-player cid="<CID>">, and %[1]s/oembed?url=<a /p/ or /c/ link> answers oEmbed; see %[1]s/docs/embed. Who authored a program and what rights it has, in plain language: %[1]s/authorship."####;

pub const OPEN_DESC: &str = r####"Decode a BeatScript share link and return its canonical text, its text (the program formatted, with the `#!` header lines and comments the link carried: what to edit), its CID and whether the CID verifies against the text. Takes either form: a self-sealing link (%[1]s/#<CID>.<packed>, a /c/<CID>.<packed> link, a bare #fragment or <CID>.<packed>) or a CID-only link (%[1]s/p/<CID>, /c/<CID>, #<CID>, or a bare CID; the same forms on an older host open too), which is resolved from the programs the site has stored (shared signed in, or rendered) and the examples, and answers its rights in meta. Either way the text is recompiled and its CID recomputed: a program whose CID is not its text's is refused with cid_mismatch, and an unknown CID answers not_found. Do not trust or play a refused text. Comments and header lines carried in a link are not covered by the CID (a beats link's notes are unverified). Read-only, no sign-in."####;

pub const RENDER_DESC: &str = r####"Make a program streamable as audio. Give exactly one of text (the program), cid (a program already stored, e.g. what an earlier render or a signed-in share returned) or example (a name from list_examples). A text or example is checked, then stored publicly under its CID as CC0-1.0 if it is not stored yet (never submit anything private; programs that use samples or "use restricted" are refused). Answers {cid, stream_url, status_url, seconds, format}: open stream_url to listen (it streams the offline render as it renders; the first sound comes within about a second, so a client can play it at once), download the same URL for a file, and read status_url to see how far a render is. format is opus (Ogg Opus, small) or wav (16-bit stereo, plays chunk by chunk in a web page); default opus. To take the program to a DAW: format midi (a Standard MIDI File: a track per part, drums on channel 10 with General MIDI notes, the tempo map, key and section markers) or stems (a zip of a 32-bit float WAV per part and per deck that add up to the master, plus the master, the .mid and manifest.json with the CID, tempo, key and the stored license and credits; stems-opus: the same with Ogg Opus stems; stems-dry and stems-dry-opus: dry stems, each part before its deck and the master, with the control moves they leave out in manifest.json); a stems URL answers 202 while it is being made, ask again until it is the zip.

Call check first. The offline render plays the program as the browser's engine does, on the same engine code: the notes and sounds (kit= and sound= too) and the control lines (filters and resonance, reverb, levels, EQ, the crossfader, their ramps and lfos, the vocoder, the Wheel, moves, section and timed lines), and also what the page does not play yet (delay, crush on a deck, macro lines, duck); its notes name any line it leaves out. A program with no end (no sections or end line) is cut at the server's bar limit (256 bars). Rendering is limited per address per hour and to a few at once: on busy or rate_limited wait retry_after seconds. No sign-in needed; about %[1]d programs an hour per client."####;

pub const RECORD_DESC: &str = r####"Set up a demo recording of a program: the page in record mode (full screen code, the firing markers and follow-scroll on) and the steps that turn it into a video. Give exactly one of text, cid or example (as render does: a text or example is checked and stored publicly as CC0-1.0, nothing private). Answers {cid, seconds, record_url, record_url_manual_clock, audio_url, recipe}. record_url opens the page in record mode and plays it for a person to screen-record; record_url_manual_clock opens it on a fake clock that a script steps frame by frame (window.__btw.setTime(seconds); window.__btw.frame()), which is how site/tools/record-demo.mjs in the repo makes a perfectly synced, stutter-free MP4 (H.264, AAC, 1920x1080) plus a silent hero loop (MP4 and WebM) and a poster PNG, taking the audio from audio_url (the offline render of the same program). This tool does not capture video itself: it needs a browser and ffmpeg on your side. The audio is the offline render: notes and sounds, not control lines (filters, reverb). Call check first. Rendering limits apply (about %[1]d programs an hour per client). No sign-in needed."####;

pub const EXAMPLES_DESC: &str = r####"The public example programs of %[1]s, all CC0-1.0: name, title, description, cid, bpm, bars, license and the url of the raw .beatscript. Pass name to get that program's full text as well. Read two or three before writing a program of your own: after the cheat-sheet (%[1]s/docs/cheatsheet.md) they are the best guide to the language, from a four-line beat (first-beat) to whole tracks with sections, defs, ramps and evolve. With phrasebook true (or a meter, or syllables) it also returns the public-domain phrasebook: lines first published before 1929, each with its text, source, year, meter, stress pattern, syllables and phoneme escapes, to sing by id (sing @tempest_such_stuff on melody); choose one for its meter, see %[1]s/docs/found-phrases.md. With themes true (or a key, a role or a composer) it also returns the public-domain theme library: melodies, basses and figures composed and published before 1929, each as BeatScript bars in a stated key with the let lines and the part that play it, its work, composer, year and the themes it morphs well with; borrow one as found material or build a form from it, see %[1]s/docs/forms.md. With imports true it also lists what a `use SOURCE as NAME` line can name: the kinds of source, each template's sections (use template(passacaglia).ground as ground) and each genre template's (use genre@1 techno.drop as drop), see %[1]s/docs/BEATSCRIPT.md "Name a source: `use`". Read-only, no sign-in."####;

pub const INSTRUMENTS_DESC: &str = r####"The sounds a program may name: voices (inst=NAME), families (a name that contains one plays that family's generic voice and check warns about it), drum kits (kit=NAME) and palettes. Use only names from this answer: an unknown inst= is a check error with the nearest names. acid (a saw) and acid-square are a 303-style voice made in the engine: an accent ! is louder and opens its filter further, accents in a row build, a slide ^ glides into the next note (60 ms, or glide=), decay= is its filter's decay. Read-only, no sign-in, cached for five minutes."####;

pub const MACROS_DESC: &str = r####"The DJ macro catalogue a program's `macro` line names (docs/beatscript/ext-control.md): `macro riser on A over 8 bars` (or on the control deck, `control: macro cathedral on B over 2 bars`) fires one entry on the deck's bar line, the same thing the desktop's Effects window and the booth fire. Returns the macro@1 table, or version's: for each entry its id, label, category, family (trick, net, master, aux), transition (a one-shot), length (bars: runs `over N bars`, 1 to 64; sweep: the riser rises over them; held: held that long, then let go; own: takes no `over`), default_bars (when the line says no `over`) and needs_net (works a net deck's parts). A bare `macro` is macro@1 for good and its table never changes; a later catalogue is macro@2. listen and render play a macro line; a browser tab does not (not played here). Read-only, no sign-in."####;

pub const STOP_DESC: &str = r####"Stop what a browser tab at %[1]s is playing: the program ends and whatever its lines held (a filter, a mute) is put back. Reaches every live tab of the token's account, or one tab by session (the id the page's MCP panel shows). Needs a bearer token. No tab connected: a no_session error that says how to connect one."####;

pub const DEVICES_DESC: &str = r####"List the phones paired with the token's account (djrust on Android scans the QR code that %[1]s shows under Mobile; site/PAIRING.md): id, name, fingerprint (the code both ends showed at pairing), paired, connected (djrust has its stream open now), last_seen, and state (what it last reported: cid, playing, section, bar). Pairing is the grant: a phone paired by an account is driven over MCP with that account's token and no other.

Pass a phone's id as device to eval, load, get or stop to drive that phone alone (eval and load run the same checks as for a tab; a program with errors, samples or "use restricted" is not sent). Needs a bearer token. Read-only."####;

pub const QUEUE_DESC: &str = r####"An "up next" queue on one browser tab at %[1]s (the same tabs and rules as load: session, or device, else your most recently seen signed-in tab). When the playing program ends the page plays the next item, on the bar line where it can; a looping program (one that never ends) plays for the item's bars or minutes, else one full pass of its arrangement, and then advances. Needs a bearer token.

action is one of: list (the queue, now_playing, position, paused_reason, listener), add (items: up to %[2]d per call, %[3]d in the queue), remove (item: an item id from list, or a CID), move (item and to: reorder; to is the index in up_next, 0 plays next), clear, skip (play the next item now, or with item that item). The radio page (%[1]s/radio) shows its queue as a playlist with each row's render status, and the person there can add, remove, reorder and play rows too.

Items must already be programs: {cid} of a stored program (render and a signed-in share store theirs) or an example's CID, {example} by name from list_examples, or {url} a share link (verified exactly as load verifies it; a CID-only link must name a stored program). Optional per item: bars (1-1024) or minutes (0.1-120) to play, title. THE QUEUE NEVER COMPOSES OR CALLS A MODEL, and there is no way to make it: to queue new music, write the programs yourself (check them), store each with render or share, then add the CIDs.

The queue advances only while a listener is plausibly present, as the page reports it: it pauses at the next track boundary when the tab's audio is suspended, when the tab has been hidden with no key, pointer, touch or media key for %[4]d minutes, or after %[5]d tracks in a row without any interaction; the page then shows "Still listening? Press play to continue". Only a person on the page lifts a pause: skip is refused while paused (paused_reason says why), and load still plays one program as it always has but does not resume the queue. Writes are limited to about %[6]d a minute. The queue lives with the tab's session and is gone when the tab is."####;

/// What check, listen, generate and genre_template say about a program's references (`gen@2 techno seed=42 ...`, `seed(...)`,
/// `template(...)`, `<cid>.drop`): the definitions are beatscript_api::resolution's, the same words as docs/BEATSCRIPT.md "What a reference
/// resolves to" (the exposure test, tests/mcp_exposure.rs, holds the docs and these descriptions together).
pub fn references() -> String {
    use beatscript_api::resolution::{EXPANDED_CID_DOC, IMPORTS_DOC, PARTS_DOC, REFS_DOC, RESOLUTION_DOC};
    format!("References: a program can call the seeded generator (gen@2 techno seed=42 section=drop part=bass is the program generate prints, fitted to the program's key and tempo unless it says bpm= or key=) or borrow from seed(...), template(...) or a stored program by CID. Its cid is of the text as written (the recipe), and the answer says what the recipe makes: {REFS_DOC}; {EXPANDED_CID_DOC}; {RESOLUTION_DOC}; {PARTS_DOC}. With expand true, each entry of refs also carries its expansion (the BeatScript it is written out as where it stands), and each part its text. A diagnostic of severity info at each gen call says the CID it resolved to and the key and tempo it was fitted to; the expand tool writes the whole program out. A `use SOURCE as NAME` line names a source on a line of its own (a stored program's <cid>.drop or <cid>.drop.bass, a gen call, a canned section template(passacaglia).ground, a form, theme ode_to_joy, phrase @tempest_such_stuff, genre@1 techno.drop; docs/BEATSCRIPT.md \"Name a source: `use`\"), and the name is then used as a section, a let or a phrase of the program's own (in a role, as its source): it is a reference of kind `use` in refs, a note at it says what the name is, and {IMPORTS_DOC}.")
}

/// What genre_template's answer says of the program's references (a form is `arrange from template(...)`).
pub const TEMPLATE_REFS: &str = "The answer also has expanded_cid (the CID of the program written out: the same as cid for a program with no references) and, for a program with references (a form's template(...)), refs and resolution, as check gives them: with roles bound, the template's entry in resolution has parts, each role (at any depth: a form in a role has its own) with the CID of what it is written out as.";

/// The `expand` argument of check.
pub const EXPAND_ARG: &str = "Also give each entry of refs its expansion: the BeatScript the reference is written out as where it stands, and each of a template's parts in resolution its text (default false).";

/// Go's `fmt.Sprintf` for the verbs these templates use: `%[n]s`, `%[n]d`, `%s`, `%d` (args as strings).
pub fn fill(t: &str, args: &[&str]) -> String {
    let mut out = String::new();
    let b = t.as_bytes();
    let (mut i, mut next) = (0, 0);
    while i < b.len() {
        if b[i] == b'%' && i + 1 < b.len() {
            if b[i + 1] == b'%' {
                out.push('%');
                i += 2;
                continue;
            }
            let mut j = i + 1;
            let mut idx = None;
            if b[j] == b'[' {
                let k = t[j..].find(']').map(|k| j + k).unwrap();
                idx = Some(t[j + 1..k].parse::<usize>().unwrap() - 1);
                j = k + 1;
            }
            if matches!(b[j], b's' | b'd') {
                let n = idx.unwrap_or(next);
                out.push_str(args[n]);
                next = n + 1;
                i = j + 1;
                continue;
            }
        }
        let c = t[i..].chars().next().unwrap();
        out.push(c);
        i += c.len_utf8();
    }
    out
}

pub const PUSH_PRIVATE_DESC: &str = r####"Send a private program to one of your paired phones, end-to-end encrypted, and never stored. Use it for what %[1]s does not keep: covers, programs with local samples (`sample ... from`), `use restricted`, anything not under a Creative Commons licence. The program is sealed for the phone's own key (X25519 from its pairing key, ChaCha20-Poly1305) and handed to the phone's live connection in memory: it is not written to disk, a database or a log (only its size and the device id are logged), no CID is registered, and if the phone is not connected nothing is sent and nothing is kept (push again when it is). The phone opens it, checks it and plays it itself; samples resolve against files in the phone's own sample library, and any that are missing are reported back in get's diagnostics, never fetched. Give device and exactly one of text (sealed here, so the text is in this server's memory for this call, where it is formatted first, as eval's is) or sealed (an envelope you sealed yourself for the phone's seal_key from devices: then this server only ever sees ciphertext). Needs a bearer token; only your own paired phones."####;

/// The lexicon harvest (docs/beatscript/lexicon-harvest.md, which quotes it word for word), said once and appended to the
/// description of each tool in `tools::HARVEST_TOOLS`.
pub const LEXICON_DESC: &str = "Lexicon harvest, a side effect with no flag: when the program checks and may be kept (no samples or loops, no `use restricted`, public visibility; push_private records nothing), the (word, phonemes) pairs of its lyrics are recorded to grow the CC0 lexicon: each annotated escape word=/phonemes/ (jingle=/j ii ng g uh l/ sings the escape and keeps the word) and each word no lexicon knows, with the rules' pronunciation. Only the word, its phonemes and the program's CID are kept, never the text. The answer's lexicon is {harvested, new}: the pairs taken from this program, and how many no program had given before. When check says a word is not in the lexicon and it sounds wrong, write it as word=/phonemes/.";

/// The `station` tool (radio, docs/beatscript/radio.md): `%[1]s` is the site's origin.
pub const STATION_DESC: &str = r####"Radio: a station is a BeatScript program that names a run of tracks with no end, and this tool answers what is at a position of one. Stations are endless and deterministic: position N of a station is always the same track (the same text, so the same CID), worked out from the station's text alone, with no clock and no store; nothing is stored here. A station program holds its `tempo`, its `key` and one block:

tempo 128
key am
station {
  from gen@2 techno seed=42
  next seed=~
  forms seeded passacaglia rondo fugue
}

from is the first track's call of the seeded generator (gen@V GENRE seed=N, optionally length=short|standard|long; the station's tempo and key are its own lines, never the call's bpm= or key=). next is the walk from one track's seed to the next: seed=+N counts on (each track's seed is the one before's plus N), seed=~ draws each seed from the CID of the track before (worked out step by step, so a position past the walk's cap is refused; a seed=+N station has a track at any position); default seed=+1. forms is the forms rotation within the genre: track N plays forms[N mod R] of the R forms (seeded, the generator's own arrangement, or passacaglia, canon, rondo, fugue, theme-and-variations, cross), each form's first role bound to the chapter's theme seed (the first track of each pass of the rotation, so it recurs) and its second role to track N's own seed; default forms seeded. The segue from track N to N+1 is the cross form of their two seeds: a program of its own, with its own CID.

Answers {ok, version, station {text, cid, genre, generator, seed, step, forms, tempo, key, length, start_cid}, n, tracks[{n, seed, theme, form, phrase, text, cid, parent, station, check}], segues[{from, to, form, phrase, text, cid, check}], neighbours, retuned, locks, diagnostics}; ok:false with error and diagnostics is an answer, not a tool error. Each track's text is a program to check, listen to, eval, render or share; its parent is track N-1's CID. A listener does not steer a station: they turn its knobs (genre, seed, step, forms, tempo, key, length), and turning one moves them to a neighbouring station that already exists. retune sets knobs before anything else, each to a value or to "+1" / "-1" for one notch (seed by 1, tempo by 2, key a fifth round the circle in its own mode, step between +1 and ~, forms rotated by one, length one step; a genre only by name), and retune keeps N: the answer is track N of the retuned station. locks are knobs held fixed: retuning a locked knob is refused, and neighbours (true) lists the stations one notch away along each unlocked knob, each with its text, its CID and its track at n. check (true) checks each track and segue: {ok, expanded_cid, bars, seconds, errors}; each program checked counts as one call of the checker. check of a station program answers station too. Read-only, no sign-in. Stations, the walk and the knobs: %[1]s/docs/radio.md."####;

/// The `eject` input of `expand`.
pub const EXPAND_EJECT: &str = "Optional: \"line:col\" (1-based) of one reference (a gen@N call, a seed(...), a <cid>.drop, a template(...)): eject it, writing just that one out in place under its `# from: <call> -> <cid>` line, the rest left as they are.";

/// The `roles` input of `genre_template`.
pub const GENRE_TEMPLATE_ROLES: &str = "Optional, with form (docs/beatscript/ext-roles.md): the form's roles, each bound to a source, {\"ROLE\": \"SOURCE\"}: a seed of the seeded generator (gen@1 dubstep seed=54876, gen@2 techno seed=42 section=drop part=bass), a public-domain theme id (ode_to_joy), notes in brackets, a stored program's part or section by CID, a stored set by CID (its tracks in order) or one of its tracks (<cid>.track2), another form (template(passacaglia, ground=gen@1 dubstep seed=54876)) or one section of it (template(cross).both, template(cross).both.arp: where a line goes, the notes its part plays). cross takes a and b, and one part of either alone as a.bass, a.melody, a.harmony; quodlibet a, b, ground and third; passacaglia ground and upper; canon subject and ground, and the numbers delay (beats behind, {\"delay\": \"6\"}) and interval (semitones, {\"interval\": \"7\"}); rondo refrain and episodes; fugue subject and counter; theme-and-variations theme. A seed is fitted to the form's key and tempo and stays an engine call, pinned (see resolution).";

/// What `genre_template` says of a form's roles (the tool's description; one source with docs/beatscript/ext-roles.md).
pub const TEMPLATE_ROLES: &str = "A form takes its sources as roles (docs/beatscript/ext-roles.md): with form and roles, each role is bound to a source (a seed of the seeded generator, a public-domain theme, notes, a stored program's part or section, another form), and the forms include cross (a quodlibet of two sources, roles a and b): two seeds that lead in turn, overlap, and morph toward each other and back.";
